      "The thing he broke from was not stupid, and it had held for two thousand years. For almost the whole history of the West, serious people assumed that values (good and evil, right and wrong, meaning, purpose) had a *foundation*. Something outside us guaranteed them. For most of that history the guarantor was God: an act was good because it accorded with the will or the order of a real divine being, and that being underwrote the whole moral structure the way bedrock underwrites a building. When God's authority started to loosen, the Enlightenment did not abandon the demand for a foundation; it looked for a replacement. Reason would ground morality (Kant had argued the moral law was carved into every rational mind), or Nature would (the way things naturally are tells us how we ought to live), or History would march toward it. The shared assumption under all of these, religious and secular alike, was that our values are *anchored*: that 'good' points at something real and external, not just at what we happen to prefer.",
