          "p": "The genealogical method applied to its most explosive material, in Nietzsche's most sustained and systematic book: *On the Genealogy of Morality* (1887). The move: do not ask whether 'good' and 'evil' are *true*; ask where they *came from*, who invented them, in what condition, and what they were really doing. The First Essay of the *Genealogy* runs that investigation on the very words 'good' and 'evil,' and its answer is that those words have a hidden history most of us would rather not look at. Understanding it requires holding two different value systems apart, because Nietzsche's whole claim is that they have different parents."
